{%- comment -%}
  Focus-mode styling, driven by `data-flow="focus"` on <html>.

  These are rendered from theme settings rather than hardcoded so the strip can
  be tuned without editing CSS — and so each removal is a decision someone made,
  not a default nobody reviewed.

  Deliberately NOT hidden in focus mode:
    · the age gate
    · the responsibility statement and the 21+ purchase note
    · privacy policy and terms links in the footer legal row
  Those are compliance obligations. Removing them would lift conversion and be
  the wrong trade.
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- if settings.flow_enabled -%}
{% style %}
  {%- if settings.flow_hide_nav -%}
  /* Header keeps its logo and buy button; the links and burger go. */
  :root[data-flow="focus"] .header__nav,
  :root[data-flow="focus"] .header__toggle,
  :root[data-flow="focus"] .header__mobile { display: none !important; }
  :root[data-flow="focus"] .header__logo { pointer-events: none; }
  {%- endif -%}

  {%- if settings.flow_hide_footer_nav -%}
  /* Footer keeps brand + legal; the link columns go. */
  :root[data-flow="focus"] .footer__cols { display: none !important; }
  :root[data-flow="focus"] .footer__top {
    grid-template-columns: 1fr !important;
    padding-bottom: 1.5rem;
  }
  {%- endif -%}

  {%- if settings.flow_hide_secondary_cta -%}
  :root[data-flow="focus"] .hero__secondary,
  :root[data-flow="focus"] .hero__scroll { display: none !important; }
  {%- endif -%}

  {%- if settings.flow_hide_announcement -%}
  :root[data-flow="focus"] .announce { display: none !important; }
  {%- endif -%}

  /* Outbound social links are the single biggest leak on an ad landing —
     the visitor came FROM social, sending them back is a guaranteed loss. */
  :root[data-flow="focus"] .footer__social { display: none !important; }

  {%- comment -%}
    ---- section stripping ------------------------------------------------

    An ad click is not a browse. Someone who just watched a fifteen-second reel
    has one decision in front of them, and every section between them and the
    buy button is a chance to leave instead.

    Three of these are not close calls:

      serve          Post-purchase content. You read how to make a martini
                     AFTER you own the bottle.
      email_capture  A COMPETING conversion. Someone who joins the list instead
                     of buying is a lost sale on traffic you already paid for —
                     the single most expensive thing on an ad landing.
      craft_process  Long-form brand story. It earns its place on the full site
                     and costs scroll depth on an ad flow.

    Kept, because each supports the decision rather than delaying it: the hero
    (the offer), the product story (why it is different, plus a second buy
    button), and social proof (credibility).

    HOW FAR TO STRIP IS ITSELF TESTABLE. Fill in a channel's "arm B" list and
    register an experiment named `<channel>_strip` scoped to that channel, and
    the two lists become the two arms. Leave arm B blank and the channel just
    uses its single list, with no experiment overhead.

    The arm-A selector is `:not([data-ab-<id>="b"])` rather than `="a"`, which
    matters: with the experiment unregistered, parked, or held out, the
    attribute is absent and `="a"` would match nothing — so a paid visitor
    would see the FULL site. Negating arm B fails toward the strip instead.

    Rules are emitted per state rather than as a blanket rule plus overrides,
    because un-hiding would need `display: revert`, which throws away whatever
    display value the section actually wanted.
  {%- endcomment -%}

  {%- liquid
    assign strip_base = settings.flow_hide_sections | default: ''
    assign channels = 'ig,tt,fb' | split: ','
  -%}

  {%- comment -%} Focus traffic with no channel resolved. {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- assign list = strip_base | split: ',' -%}
  {%- for name in list -%}
    {%- assign n = name | strip -%}
    {%- if n != blank -%}
  :root[data-flow="focus"]:not([data-channel]) [data-section-name="{{ n }}"] { display: none !important; }
    {%- endif -%}
  {%- endfor -%}

  {%- for ch in channels -%}
    {%- liquid
      assign key_a = 'flow_hide_sections_' | append: ch
      assign key_b = key_a | append: '_b'
      assign list_a = settings[key_a] | default: ''
      assign list_b = settings[key_b] | default: ''
      if list_a == blank
        assign list_a = strip_base
      endif
      assign exp_id = ch | append: '_strip'
    -%}

    {%- if list_b == blank -%}
      {%- comment -%} No test on this channel — one list, one rule set. {%- endcomment -%}
      {%- assign items = list_a | split: ',' -%}
      {%- for name in items -%}
        {%- assign n = name | strip -%}
        {%- if n != blank -%}
  :root[data-flow="focus"][data-channel="{{ ch }}"] [data-section-name="{{ n }}"] { display: none !important; }
        {%- endif -%}
      {%- endfor -%}
    {%- else -%}
      {%- comment -%}
        Arm A also covers "no experiment running", via the negation above.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      {%- assign items = list_a | split: ',' -%}
      {%- for name in items -%}
        {%- assign n = name | strip -%}
        {%- if n != blank -%}
  :root[data-flow="focus"][data-channel="{{ ch }}"]:not([data-ab-{{ exp_id }}="b"]) [data-section-name="{{ n }}"] { display: none !important; }
        {%- endif -%}
      {%- endfor -%}
      {%- assign items = list_b | split: ',' -%}
      {%- for name in items -%}
        {%- assign n = name | strip -%}
        {%- if n != blank -%}
  :root[data-flow="focus"][data-channel="{{ ch }}"][data-ab-{{ exp_id }}="b"] [data-section-name="{{ n }}"] { display: none !important; }
        {%- endif -%}
      {%- endfor -%}
    {%- endif -%}
  {%- endfor -%}

  /* ---- per channel ----------------------------------------------------
     The three platforms do not need the same treatment, so the strip is not
     applied at one uniform strength.

     Facebook carries the most desktop traffic and the most considered buyers.
     Stripping a desktop page to a single column reads as a broken site rather
     than a focused one, so the nav comes back above the mobile breakpoint. */
  @media (min-width: 900px) {
    :root[data-flow="focus"][data-channel="fb"] .header__nav { display: flex !important; }
  }

  /* TikTok and Instagram arrive on a phone, in an in-app browser, from a video.
     Everything above the buy button is time spent not deciding, so the fold is
     tightened rather than merely de-cluttered. */
  @media (max-width: 899px) {
    :root[data-flow="focus"][data-channel="tt"] .hero__sub,
    :root[data-flow="focus"][data-channel="ig"] .hero__sub { display: none; }
    :root[data-flow="focus"][data-channel="tt"] .hero__inner { padding-block-start: 0.5rem; }
  }

  /* In-app browsers put their own chrome over the bottom of the viewport, which
     sits exactly where a sticky buy bar goes. Lift it clear, and respect the
     safe-area inset so it clears the home indicator too. */
  :root[data-webview] .stickybar {
    padding-bottom: calc(0.75rem + env(safe-area-inset-bottom, 0px) + 14px);
  }
{% endstyle %}
{%- endif -%}
